│  1 What does this part of the code do?                             │
│                                                                    │
│ The function _normalize_property takes as input a property name    │
│ and its definition and returns a normalized dictionary             │
│ representing the property definition. This normalization process   │
│ includes:                                                          │
│                                                                    │
│  • If the property definition is not a dictionary, it returns a    │
│    default dictionary with type "string" and a description.        │
│  • If the property type is "object" and it has "properties", it    │
│    recursively normalizes the schema using normalize_schema.       │
│  • If the property contains a "$ref", it returns the property      │
│    definition as is.                                               │
│  • Otherwise, it makes a copy of the property, sets default values │
│    for the "type" key (defaulting to "string") and "description"   │
│    key if they are missing.                                        │
│                                                                    │
│  2 What is the relationship between this part of the code and the  │
│    bug?                                                            │
│                                                                    │
│ According to the bug description, when the MCP client normalizes   │
│ tools, the function _normalize_property is responsible for setting │
│ the default type to "string". The issue arises when the property   │
│ definition contains an "anyOf" key, which represents a union type  │
│ like List[str] | None. The current function does not check for     │
│ this "anyOf" key and unconditionally sets type to "string". This   │
│ causes tool input validation errors since the input schema's type  │
│ should not default to "string" when the property is of a union     │
│ type.                                                              │
│                                                                    │
│  3 Given the issue description, what would be the intended         │
│    behavior of this part of the code?                              │
│                                                                    │
│ The intended behavior would be to check if the property definition │
│ contains "anyOf". If it does, then the function should omit        │
│ setting a default "type" value to "string". This will prevent      │
│ incorrectly assigning "string" as the type for union types that    │
│ might include arrays, objects, or nulls. Essentially, the function │
│ _normalize_property should check for the presence of "anyOf"       │
│ before setting this default type, as the issue describes.          │
